                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d203314e-badb-33d8-8d8d-f3f770e8c09c",
                        "entry_id": "ec1b4514-58bd-37ef-8d48-acc902709c91",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Représentation d’un serpent ou d’un dragon qui se mord la queue que l’on retrouve dans de multiples cultures."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c5922850-d215-34a8-bfb4-07f5b4955ef3",
                        "entry_id": "ec1b4514-58bd-37ef-8d48-acc902709c91",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Quelque chose qui revient sur lui-même, dont le développement amène à un retour à la situation initiale."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9f2c58fb-aabc-39f1-8742-37d1c2d3dae2",
                        "entry_id": "ec1b4514-58bd-37ef-8d48-acc902709c91",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Meuble représentant l’être du même nom dans les armoiries. Il est généralement représenté la tête tournée à dextre et positionnée vers le chef. Quelques auteurs ont utilisé cette figure comme symbole pour la notion d’éternité et utilisent ce terme pour blasonner."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "75104e72-d620-442a-9328-9049ceab5eee",
                                "sense_id": "9f2c58fb-aabc-39f1-8742-37d1c2d3dae2",
                                "text": "De gueules à la tête de maure de sable tortillée du champ, enclose dans un ouroboros contourné d’or, à la champagne d’argent chargée de deux trangles ondées de sinople, qui est de Cabestany des Pyrénées-Orientales → voir illustration « armoiries avec un ouroboros »"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "908c2073-da47-4b25-bac4-302ff121702f",
                        "source_entry_id": "ec1b4514-58bd-37ef-8d48-acc902709c91",
                        "target_lang_word": "Ouroboros",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Ouroboros",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "040f2387-3a59-3ff0-9a99-8daa544aeaf8",
                                        "entry_id": "015649df-f9ba-3d35-ac29-64eaba8285f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Bildsymbol einer Schlange oder eines Drachen, die sich in den eigenen Schwanz beißt und so mit ihrem Körper einen geschlossenen Kreis bildet"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b72c866e-a6af-4d74-82de-860f0c4cbce6",
                                                "sense_id": "040f2387-3a59-3ff0-9a99-8daa544aeaf8",
                                                "text": "„Hanja wusste, dass die Welt sich drehen und sich selbst in den Schwanz beißen konnte wie die Schlange Ouroboros.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ba759fa5-2d9b-4c70-815b-d7565f318998",
                        "source_entry_id": "ec1b4514-58bd-37ef-8d48-acc902709c91",
                        "target_lang_word": "ouroboros",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "ouroboros",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "022f9890-5114-32b7-a9aa-1252a5a9295e",
                                        "entry_id": "e8cfd5be-ed3a-3d28-9ad7-b694413a41e9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A serpent, dragon or worm that eats its own tail, a representation of the continuous cycle of life and death."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c29a45b0-f466-456c-8bb7-a8ef77de8220",
                                                "sense_id": "022f9890-5114-32b7-a9aa-1252a5a9295e",
                                                "text": "The alchemists were fond of picturing their opus as a circulatory process, as a circular distillation or as the uroboros, the snake biting its own tail, and they made innumerable pictures of this process."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "04f5b261-cb55-4b5f-89e6-1d17c7cc1771",
                                                "sense_id": "022f9890-5114-32b7-a9aa-1252a5a9295e",
                                                "text": "Khepera eating his own seed is a model of Romantic creativity, where the self is isolated and sexually dual. Khepera bent over himself is a uroboros, the serpent eating its own tail, a magic circle of regeneration and rebirth. The uroboros is the prehistoric track of natural cycle, from which Judaism and Hellenism make a conceptual break."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2223f671-d614-4f66-aacd-c4c8f9aff45f",
                                                "sense_id": "022f9890-5114-32b7-a9aa-1252a5a9295e",
                                                "text": "One myth speaks of Ouroboros, a serpent-like creature that survived and regenerated itself by eating only its own tail. By neither taking from nor adding to its environment, this creature was said to be completely environmentally benign and self-sufficient."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5b5f90f7-07d3-4bee-92a2-32bba3e68bb1",
                                                "sense_id": "022f9890-5114-32b7-a9aa-1252a5a9295e",
                                                "text": "First, the snake has not caught its tail—the ouroboros figure is uncompleted. Blake executed fully formed ouroboros figures for the verso of this Night Thoughts page and for a later passage (6:690-92), and was familiar with numerous full ouroboros figures from contemporary and earlier sources […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "72f0dffc-c157-313d-a013-c49b5b9a154e",
                                        "entry_id": "e8cfd5be-ed3a-3d28-9ad7-b694413a41e9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Anything of a circular or recursive nature."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bdf58f60-a711-494b-870c-c24b457c9563",
                                                "sense_id": "72f0dffc-c157-313d-a013-c49b5b9a154e",
                                                "text": "Like an ouroboros, the story created and informed by the writer’s own experience suddenly flipped back on itself, Childress’s life now reflecting the story rather than the other way around."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "56784175-8d22-45cf-80a8-a2163c768dcf",
                                                "sense_id": "72f0dffc-c157-313d-a013-c49b5b9a154e",
                                                "text": "The result is a statistical ouroboros: a self-reinforcing discrimination machine that amplifies social inequalities under the guise of technical neutrality."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
